#{[17592186411869 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411869 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411869 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411869 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=5$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=10$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|2}=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411869 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a726a32f-5782-5170-bbe8-758da98cd984" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411869 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=5$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=10$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ | $\\frac{5}{9}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=5$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |\n| $X=10$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)]-{\\mu }_{X|2}^{2} \\\\  & =[{5}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}+{10}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}]-{8}^{2} \\\\  & =10+60-64 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411870 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411870 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411870 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411870 #edb/ref 1071 "Identify the surface ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-6x-2z+8=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411870 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b34cb85d-113c-5f8c-bb3f-86df801d970e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411870 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we complete the square in the $x$ and $z$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-6x-2z+8 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x)+4{y}^{2}-({z}^{2}+2z)+8 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+4{y}^{2}-(z+1)^{2}+1+8 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4{y}^{2}-(z+1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of an elliptic cone centered at $(3,0,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411871 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411871 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411871 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411871 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X<Y\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411871 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd343afe-c54e-593d-8dc2-ed57fc5e3a91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411871 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<Y\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =P(X<\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411872 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411872 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411872 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411872 #edb/ref 1071 "The table below gives the values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p,$ where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. Find the value of $z$ such that $P(Z\\le z)=0.01.$\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411872 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e36b6ce2-87d8-527f-bbbc-2bd96f3e28ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411872 #edb/ref 1074 "From the table, notice that\n\n$$\nP(Z\\le 2.3263)=0.99.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nP(Z\\ge 2.3263)=1-0.99=0.01.\n$$\n\nBy the symmetry of the standard normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-2.3263)=0.01.\n$$\n\n![](images/22/2212ee9de7a440c5d61814d1b2b50a249849756a4aa331e4f48180c67f95944f.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-2.3263) & =0.01 \\\\ P(Z\\le -2.3263) & =0.01.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required value of $z$ is $-2.3263.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411873 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411873 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162818" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411873 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411873 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x-2y-2z$ on the intersection of the plane $x+y+z=2$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411873 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4bd89e0-666c-5b6d-a3d4-7d170f1093d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411873 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x-2y-2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x+y+z=2,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,0,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ -2=\\lambda  \\\\ -2=\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda =-2 \\\\ \\mu z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction. So, from the third equation above, we must have $z=0.$\n\nNext, we substitute $z=0$  into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $z=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ 1+y+0 & =2 \\\\ 1+y & =2 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $z=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2 \\\\ -1+y+0 & =2 \\\\ -1+y & =2 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,1,0),\\,(-1,3,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,1,0)$ | $(-1,3,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $-1$ | $-7$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,3,0),$ and its  minimum value is $-7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9a/9a66f9263c9009f634668e826f0a84320103589b9c270360d7b2465c71e74592.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}2xz\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea08d184-a351-56ae-9a07-8c580e0db18b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411874 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/49/49a3333a52a6b7d7451095dbc46ab1009df491c92726436929a6f93cff38db60.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/df/dfa0a80bc323401f050d1302dc42c92558d8b566d9b0f985e955d34eb5cc15bd.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 3-2x}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2xz\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-2x}2xz\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3-2x}2xz\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{3-2x}2xz\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[x{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=3-2x}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(3-2x)^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3})\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3})\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3})\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}){[y]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2(9x-12{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(18x-24{x}^{2}+8{x}^{3})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(18x-24{x}^{2}+8{x}^{3})\\,dx & ={[9{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}+2{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =[9-8+2] \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
